WI-jidij proposed a declared factory->receiver-type map on the hypothesis that
real code writes `conn = sqlite3.connect(p); conn.execute(q)` rather than
constructing the type. The item required a corpus measurement BEFORE the map was
built, because a generated reach probe had already been mistaken for a payoff
once in this area. This is that measurement, and it refutes the hypothesis.

METHOD. Ground truth is `ast`, deliberately NOT the analyzer: the question is
"what does real code contain", and asking hypergumbo would answer "what can
hypergumbo see", which is the thing under test. Import-aware; tracks local
bindings AND `self.<attr>` bindings; splits production vs test with production's
own is_test_file. Cohort: hypergumbo, pretix, superset, mlflow, litellm, fastapi,

RESULT, three shapes counted separately because each needs a different fix:

    CTOR     c = smtplib.SMTP(h); c.sendmail()      192 sites (132 production)
    FACTORY  c = sqlite3.connect(p); c.execute()     29 sites  (21 production)
    MANAGER  Model.objects.filter()               4,690 sites

*** 22 OF THE 29 FACTORY SITES ARE HYPERGUMBO'S OWN TRACKER CACHE. *** Excluding
self-bias: SEVEN sites across six repos, exactly ONE on a production path.
Per-repo production factory sites: pretix 0, superset 0, mlflow 0, litellm 1,
sqlite3.connect 4 bindings cohort-wide, asyncio.open_connection ZERO,
multiprocessing.Pipe ZERO. A hand-written, hand-maintained map serving one
external production site is not worth the maintenance surface. Item closed
wont_do with the measurement attached.

THE POSITIVE CONTROL FAILED ON ITS FIRST RUN, AND THAT IS WHY THE NULL IS
BELIEVABLE. The scanner tracked only local `Name` bindings, so it could not see
`self._conn = sqlite3.connect(...)` — an INSTANCE ATTRIBUTE, which is how a
long-lived connection is actually written and the exact shape of the control file
(this repo's own tracker cache). Without the control the corpus would have
returned near-zero and a real item would have been closed on a scanner bug.
Fixed with a two-pass class scan; the control now finds 3 bindings and 20
catalogued method sites.

A CLAIM MADE WRONG AND CORRECTED THE SAME DAY, recorded because the shape
recurs. The first write-up called the Django MANAGER shape "not modelled at all".
It is modelled — WI-sozoj at py.py:5734, gated on `.objects.` plus a closed
method set — and pretix yields 846 django-attributed chains with
`django.db.models.filter` (447) the largest single primitive in the repo. This
scanner had reused WI-sozoj's OWN `.objects.` discriminator, so it reproduced a
shipped feature's rule and then reported it as a gap. Also corrected: 4,690 is a
SITE count and 846 is a CHAIN count (deduplicated per boundary/primitive/src), so
no ratio between them is claimed — sites, chains and walks are different
populations. Both corrections are written into the instrument's docstring so the
next reader inherits them.

